{"name":"Willow Leaf","hex":"#a1a46d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"willow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various deciduous trees or shrubs in the genus Salix, in the willow family Salicaceae, found primarily on moist soils in cooler zones in the northern hemisphere.","The wood of these trees."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To open and cleanse (cotton, flax, wool, etc.) by means of a willow.","To form a shape or move in a way similar to the long, slender branches of a willow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/willow"},{"term":"leaf","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The usually green and flat organ that represents the most prominent feature of most vegetative plants.","A foliage leaf or any of the many and often considerably different structures it can specialise into."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To produce leaves; put forth foliage.","To divide (a vegetable) into separate leaves."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leaf"}],"entries":[{"title":"Willow","blocks":[{"text":"Willows, also called sallows and osiers, of the genus Salix, comprise around 350 species of typically deciduous trees and shrubs. They are primarily found on moist soils in cold and temperate regions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Salix_alba_Morton.jpg/330px-Salix_alba_Morton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Willow","wikidata":"Q36050"},{"title":"Leaf","blocks":[{"text":"A leaf is a principal appendage of the stem of a vascular plant, usually borne laterally above ground and specialized for photosynthesis. Leaves are collectively called foliage, as in \"autumn foliage\", while the leaves, stem, flower, and fruit collectively form the shoot system. In most leaves, the primary photosynthetic tissue is the palisade mesophyll and is located on the upper side of the blade or lamina of the leaf, but in some species, including the mature foliage of Eucalyptus, palisade mesophyll is present on both sides and the leaves are said to be isobilateral. The leaf is an integral part of the stem system, and most leaves are flattened and have distinct upper (adaxial) and lower (abaxial) surfaces that differ in color, hairiness, the number of stomata, the amount and structure of epicuticular wax, and other features. Leaves are mostly green in color due to the presence of a compound called chlorophyll, which is essential for photosynthesis as it absorbs light energy from the Sun. A leaf with lighter-colored or white patches or edges is called a variegated leaf."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Lisc_lipy.jpg/330px-Lisc_lipy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf","wikidata":"Q33971"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg/500px-Salix_silesiaca_a5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"At the base of the petiole, a pair of stipules form. These may fall in spring, or last for much of the summer or even for more than one year (marcescence).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASalix_silesiaca_a5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Young-catkin.jpg/500px-Young-catkin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Young male catkin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoung-catkin.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://